  {
    "episode_id": 75001,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75001.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, I've been a proponent of decriminalizing marijuana since I was a senator in the early 80s. In fact, before that, I was a police legal advisor for the city of Memphis in the 70s. And I work with the state representative on the citation a little of arrest law. And we made it clear after I'd helped him draw it and lobbied for it. And he was a state rep and sponsored it. And then we made clear that the offenses it would be used for be any misdemeanors, which would include possession of marijuana. And then we got that started in the Memphis Police Department where unless you could so circumstances were as necessary to make a physical arrest a citation would be issued. And then later in the State Senate, we've tried and continued to Congress to change. Nobody should lose their liberty, lose the opportunity of a scholarship, lose the opportunity of federal housing, or not get a job because of marijuana possession. It's no different, I guess, than drinking alcohol in a dry jurisdiction, which shouldn't be such either, but there are such. The penalty was absurd. medical marijuana has shown, and I've seen it for years, at first I guess I had a girlfriend die in 1980 of cancer, and marijuana helped her with the chemotherapy that she was going through. And then I had a pal who was a former seal who died of pancreatic cancer, and in 1990, the seal was the 96th campaign we came up to me, so it must have been a 97 when we was dying in Maryland. Of course it wasn't legal. But his mother, who was in her middle lady, said, thank God for the marijuana. It's the only thing that allows oral to smile or eat. And he'd gone from 210, and the guy you wanted on your side, and if you had a problem, somewhere to be in 120 pounds. And he spoke marijuana, he smiled, he laughed, and he ate. So it helps people with cancer, it helps people with chemo, and it helps people with pain, and it helps veterans with PTSD. It helps people with Parkinson's as well.",
